  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q03-10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "True or False? Which of the following statements are always true?",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q03",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\log(x^2)=2\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "The left-hand side is defined for $x\\ne0$, but the right-hand side requires $x>0$. For example, at $x=-1$, $\\log(x^2)=\\log(1)=0$, while $\\log(x)$ is undefined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q04",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\log(x^3)=3\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Both sides are defined exactly when $x>0$. On this domain, the power law gives $\\log(x^3)=3\\log(x)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q05",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\log(x^4)=4\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "The left-hand side is defined for $x\\ne0$, but the right-hand side requires $x>0$. At $x=-1$, the left-hand side equals $\\log(1)=0$, while the right-hand side is undefined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q06",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\log(x^5)=5\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "The condition $x^5>0$ is equivalent to $x>0$, so both sides have the same domain. The power law is valid throughout that domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q07",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\log(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac12\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Both logarithmic expressions require $x>0$. On this domain,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log(\\sqrt{x})&=\\log(x^{1/2})\\\\\\log(\\sqrt{x})&=\\tfrac12\\log(x)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q08",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\log(\\sqrt[3]{x})=\\frac13\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Although a real cube root exists for negative inputs, its logarithm requires $\\sqrt[3]{x}>0$, so $x>0$. On this domain,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log(\\sqrt[3]{x})&=\\log(x^{1/3})\\\\\\log(\\sqrt[3]{x})&=\\tfrac13\\log(x)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q09",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$\\log\\left(\\frac1x\\right)=-\\log(x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Both sides require $x>0$. Using the quotient law,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log\\left(\\frac1x\\right)&=\\log(1)-\\log(x)\\\\\\log\\left(\\frac1x\\right)&=-\\log(x)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q10",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$\\log(x^2)=2\\log|x|$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Both sides require $x\\ne0$. Since $|x|>0$ on this domain, the power law applies to $|x|$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log(x^2)&=\\log(|x|^2)\\\\\\log(x^2)&=2\\log|x|\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
